WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.495
This is eleven years of daily returns from the South African stock market.

00:00:04.945 --> 00:00:07.345
Most days, the line barely moves.

00:00:07.795 --> 00:00:09.308
But look at 2008.

00:00:09.758 --> 00:00:12.904
The swings get large, and they stay large for months.

00:00:13.354 --> 00:00:17.075
Big moves follow big moves, and calm follows calm.

00:00:17.525 --> 00:00:20.160
Economists call this volatility clustering.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:26.449
If we trace the typical size of the moves over the last twenty days, the clusters become obvious.

00:00:26.899 --> 00:00:28.995
And that raises a practical question.

00:00:29.445 --> 00:00:33.668
If volatility clusters, then today should tell us something about tomorrow.

00:00:34.118 --> 00:00:36.554
Can we forecast tomorrow's volatility?

00:00:37.004 --> 00:00:46.672
And a more specific question, from a 2016 paper we set out to test: does first passing the returns through a wavelet transform make those forecasts better?
